WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.759
Okay, so onto the main topic of the day, which is about how you best use these kinds

00:05.759 --> 00:10.560
of advanced coding agents if you're working on a large team with a big code base.

00:10.560 --> 00:15.640
And this is where historically they've been criticized, because things like Cloud Code

00:15.640 --> 00:21.440
work really well with great market data demos like the one you just saw, when there's a nice,

00:21.440 --> 00:27.519
defined, small project it's building from scratch, it can write the docs, where they can struggle

00:27.519 --> 00:34.399
is when they're suddenly inheriting a massive code base. And to be honest, this is less true

00:34.399 --> 00:40.560
now, now that we are past the point of inflection from last November, now they are much better

00:40.560 --> 00:46.000
at handling massive code bases. But there are still rules of the road, there are still some good

00:46.000 --> 00:49.119
practices, which allow you to be successful as a large

00:49.119 --> 00:52.240
team and I'd like to go through them with you now. And I should warn you that in most

00:52.240 --> 00:56.640
cases I'm really stating the obvious here. I feel like by this point in the course you already

00:56.640 --> 01:02.000
know this stuff pretty well. We're basically talking about focusing on the top half of those

01:02.000 --> 01:11.000
kind of six ways of doing things on the yellow, on the 2025 techniques more than the purple 2026 crazy techniques.

01:11.239 --> 01:16.159
So first of all, invest time in your agents.md or your

01:16.159 --> 01:20.920
Claude.md in your documentation, which is disclosed

01:20.920 --> 01:24.760
progressively only as you get to the subdirectories. Make

01:24.760 --> 01:28.519
sure that at every level, at every subdirectory, there is

01:28.519 --> 01:32.519
great documentation that it reflects the interface.

01:32.519 --> 01:38.920
It reflects what an agent would need to know in order to work within this directory, this folder,

01:38.920 --> 01:40.840
without having to read all the files.

01:40.840 --> 01:47.920
So it should be like really a good, tight documentation that reflects the package

01:47.920 --> 01:51.000
and what's needed to be known as soon as one goes inside it.

01:51.000 --> 01:56.299
And then, you know, making sure that it is at the right level of detail, not so much detail

01:56.299 --> 02:02.000
that it consumes context, but enough so that the right core functions are called in that

02:02.000 --> 02:03.099
package.

02:03.099 --> 02:06.019
That is the trick and it takes time and effort.

02:06.019 --> 02:09.740
You can get, of course, Cloud Code to write this for you, but then you need to eyeball it

02:09.740 --> 02:11.780
and make sure you're satisfied with it.

02:11.780 --> 02:13.460
Do a couple of rounds of reviews.

02:13.460 --> 02:20.800
Invest time in your agents.md, it will pay dividends. And also, project documentation

02:20.800 --> 02:27.039
for agents is what it's all about. If you have a document that you tag with the @ sign,

02:27.039 --> 02:32.800
then that gets inserted into, in an entirety, into that outer document, which is not normally

02:32.800 --> 02:39.740
what you want. Better is to describe what a document does and then put a link to it because then Claude

02:39.740 --> 02:44.919
Code or the other coding agent can decide does it need to know about this document and

02:44.919 --> 02:50.419
if so it will read it. So structuring your documents to be summarizing and then to be

02:50.419 --> 02:56.020
pointing to more detailed documents so that an agent can navigate properly. That is a

02:56.020 --> 03:01.360
key to success with big code bases and taking time to get that documentation

03:01.360 --> 03:03.199
and making sure that it's up to date

03:03.199 --> 03:06.120
as changes happen, always make sure there's a step

03:06.120 --> 03:08.520
when documents are revised and updated.

03:08.520 --> 03:12.439
And then having a consistent strategy

03:12.439 --> 03:16.500
for how you will work as a team using coding agents.

03:16.500 --> 03:20.000
So for example, if you want to use the GitHub approach

03:20.000 --> 03:21.759
where you will tag Claude,

03:21.759 --> 03:23.919
then have that be the policy,

03:23.919 --> 03:25.919
have that be the way that you're gonna work as a team.

03:25.919 --> 03:28.039
If everyone's approaching it a bit differently,

03:28.039 --> 03:30.000
things can become a complete muddle.

03:30.000 --> 03:32.080
You're not sure who's following what process,

03:32.080 --> 03:34.360
who is it that's putting tickets in JIRA,

03:34.360 --> 03:36.800
and then you're using one flow for this,

03:36.800 --> 03:39.780
who is it that's using the GitHub actions

03:39.780 --> 03:41.039
and tag in Claude.

03:41.039 --> 03:47.520
So you wanna have one consistent flow, one agreed process that everyone follows.

03:47.520 --> 03:51.680
And if you're going to use plugins, which makes a lot of sense, like the feature dev plugin,

03:51.680 --> 03:56.159
that I guess we used a couple of weeks ago now, a week ago, then have that be something that you

03:56.159 --> 04:02.560
all agree to and you rally around and it's part of your project process. And that way you'll have

04:02.560 --> 04:06.560
common, you'll have consistency across the project and also everyone knows

04:06.560 --> 04:10.039
when you're going to order a new feature what's the right way to approach it.

04:10.039 --> 04:14.159
So when it comes to choosing things to add on for your project, I would focus on plugins

04:14.159 --> 04:19.959
first and foremost, like add the right plugins like maybe the code simplification and the

04:19.959 --> 04:23.259
feature dev and a few others that make sense for your project.

04:23.259 --> 04:29.959
When it comes to building specialized functionality for coding agents to use on your project.

04:29.959 --> 04:34.740
I recommend focusing on skills as a thing that makes so much sense right now.

04:34.740 --> 04:40.040
Develop skills, special skills that make sense for your project, how common things

04:40.040 --> 04:41.959
should work in your project.

04:41.959 --> 04:47.680
An example, this bit of an obvious example might be the cerebras skill that we wrote.

04:47.680 --> 04:52.040
If you particularly want to use that approach for LLM inference in your project,

04:52.040 --> 04:56.540
then build that out as a skill. But that's a bit of a bitty thing to mention. I'm really

04:56.540 --> 05:01.439
thinking of bigger skills you might want your agent to know. Ways in which it has to understand

05:01.439 --> 05:06.759
the frameworks that you're using on your project and making sure that that's something that has

05:06.759 --> 05:12.920
a special skill that is that is domain specific. If, for example, you were using a particular technique

05:12.920 --> 05:14.759
for market data that mattered,

05:14.759 --> 05:16.439
then you might have a skill around that.

05:16.439 --> 05:17.600
So it was very clear,

05:17.600 --> 05:20.800
the right way to use the market data API

05:20.800 --> 05:22.040
throughout your project.

05:22.040 --> 05:24.800
So building special skills is a great way

05:24.800 --> 05:27.720
to have common standards, common approaches

05:27.720 --> 05:29.639
that you know will be reinforced

05:29.639 --> 05:32.959
for all development across the project by coding agents.

05:32.959 --> 05:35.040
And the next one is stating the obvious, I'm afraid,

05:35.040 --> 05:39.639
but having a robust test suite, of course,

05:39.639 --> 05:41.439
it's like likely to always--

05:41.439 --> 05:43.079
I mean, test driven development, of course,

05:43.079 --> 05:45.519
is well known as being something

05:45.519 --> 05:49.000
that is incredibly important for large projects

05:49.000 --> 05:51.959
and having 80% unit test coverage for a long time

05:51.959 --> 05:53.680
has been the kind of gold standard.

05:53.680 --> 05:55.759
I sort of suggest, though, being

05:55.759 --> 05:56.819
less focused

05:56.819 --> 06:00.720
on things like the percentage coverage of your tests,

06:00.720 --> 06:03.519
LLMs will often focus on that,

06:03.519 --> 06:07.759
and they will often, to a fault, do things like mocking out,

06:07.759 --> 06:08.759
if you're familiar with this,

06:08.759 --> 06:12.680
that the right tests that are very, very specifically

06:12.680 --> 06:15.399
designed to make sure that every path through the code

06:15.399 --> 06:17.439
is tested, and that's not really what you want,

06:17.439 --> 06:19.740
and you certainly don't want brittle tests

06:19.740 --> 06:21.259
that are overly mocked.

06:21.259 --> 06:22.980
You want good tests.

06:22.980 --> 06:26.019
You want tests that actually test the functionality

06:26.019 --> 06:27.939
you're trying to build such that

06:27.939 --> 06:29.379
if there's some reimplementation,

06:29.379 --> 06:30.740
it doesn't break your tests.

06:30.740 --> 06:32.019
But if there's something that actually does

06:32.019 --> 06:34.540
break the logic, then it breaks the tests.

06:34.540 --> 06:37.259
So I'm making sure that you have,

06:37.259 --> 06:42.560
that you give feedback on the testing strategy, maybe as part of your agents.md.

06:42.560 --> 06:46.279
That's very important and always pushing back

06:46.279 --> 06:49.240
if you see a ton of tests getting put in there,

06:49.240 --> 06:50.879
just for the sake of testing,

06:50.879 --> 06:53.040
mocking everything out and just making sure

06:53.040 --> 06:54.720
that different parts through the code are tested.

06:54.720 --> 06:57.620
I mean, that's what you would do as a human review as well,

06:57.620 --> 06:58.720
and that's what you should do,

06:58.720 --> 07:00.800
particularly with coding agents.

07:00.800 --> 07:02.000
And there's a lot of overlap here

07:02.000 --> 07:03.399
because really that second item

07:03.399 --> 07:05.759
about having consistent workflows and plugins,

07:05.759 --> 07:08.519
that should include having the kinds of workflows

07:08.519 --> 07:11.519
and instructions to alarms that make sure

07:11.519 --> 07:14.360
that they write good tests that don't overly mock.

07:14.360 --> 07:17.240
Okay, and then finally, of course,

07:17.240 --> 07:19.800
at the end of the day, you are responsible,

07:19.800 --> 07:22.600
you are accountable for the quality of your code.

07:22.600 --> 07:25.120
Your coding agent is a tool that is helping you,

07:25.120 --> 07:30.319
you and your team. And so there needs to be a culture of there being a human reviewer,

07:30.319 --> 07:37.279
the buck stopping with the human and a strong culture of rejecting coding agent slop.

07:37.279 --> 07:43.199
If they are writing long, long files that are just going to be a hard work to review,

07:43.199 --> 07:48.879
if they're being overly defensive, all of these things need to be caught and fixed.

07:48.879 --> 07:52.000
And the, you know, there's definitely this asymmetry problem

07:52.000 --> 07:55.399
that is becoming increasingly easy to generate tons

07:55.399 --> 07:57.279
and tons of code and the,

07:57.279 --> 08:00.519
the onus is becoming on the human to then take all

08:00.519 --> 08:02.079
of this code and have to review it,

08:02.079 --> 08:03.560
which, which is hard work.

08:03.560 --> 08:04.920
It's very hard work.

08:04.920 --> 08:09.319
And, and so making sure that there are disciplined

08:09.319 --> 08:12.240
processes in place to make sure that that human review happens

08:12.439 --> 08:16.360
and making sure that coding agents are challenged to write

08:16.360 --> 08:19.480
succinct code that doesn't overwhelm the human reviewer.

08:19.639 --> 08:21.759
These are all priorities.

08:21.959 --> 08:25.439
And there's tons of other good advice and you'll see lots of articles

08:25.439 --> 08:28.959
about this stuff and some of the good advice will become no

08:28.959 --> 08:31.220
longer relevant very quickly as the models

08:31.220 --> 08:32.720
get stronger and stronger.

08:32.720 --> 08:37.700
But one of the things that I would certainly add to this list would be to always work with

08:37.700 --> 08:39.440
bite-sized chunks.

08:39.440 --> 08:44.179
When you're dealing with a massive project, you don't want to tag Claude and say, "Hey,

08:44.179 --> 08:47.279
refactor the whole codebase," because you're asking for trouble.

08:47.279 --> 08:49.000
You could do that with a small project.

08:49.000 --> 08:52.559
We could do it with our little one that we're working on right now this week.

08:52.559 --> 08:58.159
But you can't do that if you've got a massive project with 50 people working on it. So take

08:58.159 --> 09:03.360
small bits at a time, let a human or working with something like Cloud Code,

09:03.360 --> 09:09.600
divvy up a big piece of work into smaller steps, and then assign the small steps to the coding

09:09.600 --> 09:14.899
agent, have each one be something that is independently, can be specified, can be

09:14.899 --> 09:20.600
tested, can be reviewed by a human. So taking it in small steps, absolutely

09:20.600 --> 09:24.080
crucial. And this is very much an evolving story. If you have your own

09:24.080 --> 09:27.519
advice from your own experiences, please do post them in Udemy. It'll be

09:27.519 --> 09:31.279
great to have conversation about this. And if you want to have a quick

09:31.279 --> 09:35.960
assignment, one thing that's quite entertaining to do is to take a big

09:35.960 --> 09:37.379
open-source project. Maybe something from your domain, maybe something that One thing that's quite entertaining to do is to take a big open source project,

09:37.379 --> 09:41.419
maybe something from your domain, maybe something that's from whatever industry

09:41.419 --> 09:47.279
versus you work in, a popular open source platform, and then clone it and then try and take on

09:47.279 --> 09:49.720
like something that's got to do or something like that.

09:49.720 --> 09:59.919
Ask the LML to find a to do in the code and do it and see how it does. And then work on things, some of these steps here,

09:59.919 --> 10:05.360
work on a more detailed agents.md across all of the

10:05.360 --> 10:08.879
entire directory structure, work on perhaps the

10:08.879 --> 10:11.759
feature dev plugin and put some of these things into

10:11.759 --> 10:13.600
practice and see how it does.

10:13.600 --> 10:16.440
And you could also, as a sort of anti-test, you could

10:16.440 --> 10:19.879
try the kind of, hey, refactor the whole code base

10:19.879 --> 10:20.759
and see what happens.

10:20.759 --> 10:22.399
Simplify everything.

10:22.399 --> 10:24.159
You could maybe put that in a rough loop

10:24.159 --> 10:26.039
and leave it going for ten iterations.

10:26.039 --> 10:27.440
See what comes out the other end,

10:27.440 --> 10:29.120
but it's probably not gonna be pretty.

10:29.120 --> 10:31.759
And that will give you a good sense of what does work

10:31.759 --> 10:34.600
and what doesn't work with a massive code base.

